{
  "mn46:0.1": "Middle Discourses 46 ",
  "mn46:0.2": "The Great Discourse on Taking Up Practices ",
  "mn46:1.1": "So I have heard. ",
  "mn46:1.2": "At one time the Buddha was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery. ",
  "mn46:1.3": "There the Buddha addressed the mendicants, ",
  "mn46:1.4": "“Mendicants!” ",
  "mn46:1.5": "“Venerable sir,” they replied. ",
  "mn46:1.6": "The Buddha said this: ",
  "mn46:2.1": "“Mendicants, sentient beings typically have the wish, desire, and hope: ",
  "mn46:2.2": "‘Oh, if only unlikable, undesirable, and disagreeable things would decrease, and likable, desirable, and agreeable things would increase!’ ",
  "mn46:2.3": "But exactly the opposite happens to them. ",
  "mn46:2.4": "What do you take to be the reason for this?” ",
  "mn46:2.5": "“Our teachings are rooted in the Buddha. He is our guide and our refuge. Sir, may the Buddha himself please clarify the meaning of this. The mendicants will listen and remember it.” ",
  "mn46:2.6": "“Well then, mendicants, listen and apply your mind well, I will speak.” ",
  "mn46:2.7": "“Yes, sir,” they replied. ",
  "mn46:2.8": "The Buddha said this: ",
  "mn46:3.1": "“Take an unlearned ordinary person who has not seen the noble ones, and is neither skilled nor trained in the teaching of the noble ones. They’ve not seen true persons, and are neither skilled nor trained in the teaching of the true persons. ",
  "mn46:3.2": "They don’t know what practices they should